ADDART({id:"sinosauropteryx",t:"Sinosauropteryx",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦊",w:"Sinosauropteryx",r:["compsognathus","microraptor","dinobirds","archaeopteryx","fossils","birds"],
p:[["The first fuzzy dinosaur","The first non-bird dinosaur found with feathers."],["A striped tail","Its colors have been worked out from fossils."],["Small hunter","About 1 meter long, it ate lizards and small mammals."]],
l:"Sinosauropteryx was a small meat-eating dinosaur that lived about 124 million years ago, in the Early Cretaceous, in what is now Liaoning Province in northeastern China. It was about 1 meter long, most of that a very long tail, and weighed under a kilogram. In 1996, it became world-famous as the first dinosaur other than birds found with evidence of feathers: its fossils show a coat of short, simple, hair-like filaments, sometimes called ‘dinofuzz’, running along its back and tail. These early feathers could not have been used for flight; they probably helped keep the animal warm and may have been used for display. In 2010 and 2017, scientists studying microscopic pigment structures called melanosomes in its fossils found that Sinosauropteryx had a reddish-brown, or ginger, color, a striped tail with light and dark bands, and a ‘bandit mask’ of dark feathers around its eyes, along with a lighter underside, a pattern called countershading that helps camouflage animals in open habitats. Some specimens preserve lizards and small mammals in their stomachs, showing what they ate.",
s:[["A landmark discovery","A farmer, Li Yumin, found the fossil in 1996. It was named by Chinese paleontologists Ji Qiang and Ji Shu’an. Its feathery covering surprised scientists and led to the discovery of many more feathered dinosaurs in the same rocks."],["Colors from fossils","Melanosomes come in different shapes for different colors. By comparing them with those of living birds, scientists determined that Sinosauropteryx was ginger-brown with a banded tail, the first dinosaur whose colors were reconstructed."],["Camouflage","The dark back and pale belly, together with the eye mask, suggest Sinosauropteryx lived in open areas with bright light, where countershading would make it harder for predators and prey to see."],["Relatives","Sinosauropteryx was a compsognathid, a close relative of Compsognathus from Germany. Its discovery suggested that Compsognathus may also have had feathers."]],
k:[["Lived","About 124 million years ago"],["Where","Liaoning, China"],["Length","About 1 meter"],["Covering","Simple, hair-like feathers"],["Colors","Ginger with a striped tail"],["Discovered","1996"]],
d:[["1996","First feathered non-bird dinosaur described"],["2010","Pigment structures in its feathers identified"],["2017","Full color pattern reconstructed"]],
s2:[["Name","Sinosauropteryx means ‘Chinese lizard wing’, though it did not have wings for flying."],["Eggs inside","One specimen contains two eggs inside its body, showing females carried eggs in pairs like birds."]],
g:[["Feather","A structure made of keratin growing from skin"],["Melanosome","A tiny structure that holds pigment"],["Countershading","Dark on top and light below for camouflage"],["Compsognathid","A family of small meat-eating dinosaurs"]],
f:["Sinosauropteryx had feathers.","Sinosauropteryx had a striped tail.","Sinosauropteryx was found in China."],
fun:"Sinosauropteryx was ginger-colored with a striped tail, a bit like a raccoon or red panda."});

ADDART({id:"pachyrhinosaurus",t:"Pachyrhinosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Pachyrhinosaurus",r:["styracosaurus","triceratops","edmontosaurus","dino","fossils","arctic"],
p:[["A thick-nosed dinosaur","It had a bony pad instead of a nose horn."],["Arctic dinosaur","Some lived in Alaska."],["Herds","Huge bone beds hold many animals."]],
l:"Pachyrhinosaurus was a horned, plant-eating dinosaur that lived about 73.5 to 69 million years ago, in the Late Cretaceous, in what are now Alberta, Canada, and Alaska, United States. It was about 6 to 8 meters long and weighed around 3 to 4 tonnes. Its name means ‘thick-nosed lizard’: instead of a sharp nose horn like most of its relatives, adults had a large, flat, rough bony lump, called a boss, on top of the snout and over the eyes. The boss may have been covered in a thick horny layer and used for shoving contests between rivals, a little like musk oxen today. Pachyrhinosaurus also had a frill decorated with spikes and hornlets. It is known from huge bone beds containing remains of hundreds of animals of all ages, suggesting they lived and migrated in large herds. Fossils from the North Slope of Alaska show that Pachyrhinosaurus lived well north of the Arctic Circle, where it would have experienced months of darkness and cool temperatures in winter. Each region had its own species, including Pachyrhinosaurus perotorum from Alaska.",
s:[["Discovery","The first Pachyrhinosaurus skull was found in 1946 in Alberta by Charles M. Sternberg and named in 1950. Because its nose boss looked so strange, some at first thought the horn had been broken off."],["Pipestone Creek","The Pipestone Creek bone bed near Grande Prairie, Alberta, contains thousands of bones from a herd of Pachyrhinosaurus, perhaps drowned crossing a flooded river."],["Arctic life","Alaskan Pachyrhinosaurus shared their polar home with Edmontosaurus, tyrannosaurs and small predators. Scientists debate whether they migrated south in winter or stayed through the dark months."],["Movie star","Pachyrhinosaurus was the main character of the 2013 animated film Walking with Dinosaurs."]],
k:[["Lived","About 73.5–69 million years ago"],["Where","Alberta and Alaska"],["Length","About 6–8 meters"],["Weight","About 3–4 tonnes"],["Famous feature","Bony nose boss"],["Name means","Thick-nosed lizard"]],
d:[["1946","First skull found"],["1950","Pachyrhinosaurus named"],["1972","Pipestone Creek bone bed discovered"],["2011","Alaskan species named"]],
s2:[["Musk ox","Like musk oxen, Pachyrhinosaurus may have butted heads, using its thick boss to absorb impacts."],["Polar dinosaurs","Dinosaurs lived near both poles, showing they could cope with cold, dark winters."]],
g:[["Boss","A thick, rounded bony lump"],["Ceratopsian","A horned, frilled, beaked dinosaur"],["Bone bed","A layer of rock packed with fossil bones"],["Arctic Circle","The line of latitude around the North Pole region"]],
f:["Pachyrhinosaurus had a bony nose boss.","Pachyrhinosaurus lived in Alaska.","Pachyrhinosaurus lived in herds."],
fun:"Pachyrhinosaurus lived so far north that it saw months of winter darkness."});

ADDART({id:"gorgonops",t:"Gorgonopsians",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐺",w:"Gorgonopsia",r:["dimetrodon","massext","pangaea","smilodon","iceagemammals","lystrosaurus"],
p:[["Saber-toothed before dinosaurs","Fierce predators of the Permian."],["Mammal relatives","They belonged to the group leading to mammals."],["Wiped out","They died in the end-Permian extinction."]],
l:"Gorgonopsians were a group of fierce predators that lived about 265 to 252 million years ago, in the Permian Period, before the age of dinosaurs. They were synapsids, the group of animals that includes mammals and their ancestors, so they were more closely related to us than to reptiles such as lizards. Gorgonopsians had long skulls, powerful jaws that could open very wide, and long, saber-like canine teeth, which they probably used to slash and stab large prey, much as saber-toothed cats did hundreds of millions of years later. They walked with their legs held fairly upright beneath their bodies. Most were the size of large dogs, but the largest, such as Inostrancevia from Russia, reached about 3.5 meters long with canines over 12 centimeters, making them the top predators of their time. Many fossils come from the Karoo Basin of South Africa and from Russia. Gorgonopsians vanished in the end-Permian mass extinction about 252 million years ago, the greatest extinction in Earth’s history, which killed most species on land and in the sea.",
s:[["Name","The name comes from the Gorgons of Greek mythology, monsters such as Medusa whose gaze turned people to stone, referring to their fearsome appearance."],["Saber teeth","Saber teeth evolved many times in different groups, including gorgonopsians, some marsupials and the saber-toothed cats. This is an example of convergent evolution."],["Mammal-like features","Gorgonopsians had different kinds of teeth, incisors, canines and cheek teeth, like mammals, and an opening in the skull behind the eye for jaw muscles. But they still laid eggs and probably had no fur."],["Last of their kind","As the end-Permian extinction struck, perhaps caused by massive volcanic eruptions in Siberia, the largest gorgonopsians spread into new areas, but all were gone by its end."]],
k:[["Lived","About 265–252 million years ago (Permian)"],["Group","Synapsids (mammal relatives)"],["Famous feature","Saber-like canine teeth"],["Largest","Inostrancevia, about 3.5 m long"],["Fossils from","South Africa, Russia, Tanzania"],["Extinction","End-Permian mass extinction"]],
d:[["1876","Gorgonops named by Richard Owen"],["1922","Inostrancevia named from Russian fossils"],["2023","Inostrancevia fossils described from South Africa"]],
s2:[["In documentaries","A gorgonopsian appears in the 2005 TV series Walking with Monsters and the series Primeval."],["Before dinosaurs","Gorgonopsians died out about 20 million years before the first dinosaurs appeared."]],
g:[["Synapsid","An animal in the group including mammals and their ancestors"],["Canine","A long, pointed tooth"],["Permian","The period from about 299 to 252 million years ago"],["Convergent evolution","Different groups evolving similar features"]],
f:["Gorgonopsians had saber teeth.","They were relatives of mammals.","They died out in the end-Permian extinction."],
fun:"Gorgonopsians had saber teeth more than 200 million years before saber-toothed cats."});

ADDART({id:"lystrosaurus",t:"Lystrosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐷",w:"Lystrosaurus",r:["massext","pangaea","gorgonops","dimetrodon","fossils","antarc"],
p:[["The great survivor","It lived through the worst mass extinction ever."],["Everywhere afterward","It became one of the most common land animals."],["Proof of drifting continents","Fossils on many continents support plate tectonics."]],
l:"Lystrosaurus was a pig-sized plant-eating animal that lived about 255 to 250 million years ago, around the end of the Permian and start of the Triassic periods. It was a dicynodont, a kind of synapsid, distantly related to mammals, with a stocky body, short legs, a horny beak and two tusk-like teeth in its upper jaw. Most species were about 0.6 to 2.5 meters long. Lystrosaurus is famous for surviving the end-Permian mass extinction about 252 million years ago, when roughly 90 percent of species died out. In the early Triassic world that followed, with few competitors and predators, it spread across the supercontinent Pangaea and became extraordinarily common: in some rock layers, it makes up most of the fossils found. Its fossils have been found in South Africa, India, China, Russia and Antarctica, continents that are now far apart, and this was important evidence that they were once joined, supporting the theory of continental drift. Scientists think its burrowing habits, ability to cope with low oxygen, flexible diet and fast breeding may have helped it survive.",
s:[["The survivor","After the extinction, Lystrosaurus thrived in a hot, harsh world. Studies of its bone growth suggest that post-extinction Lystrosaurus grew fast and bred young, a strategy that helps animals survive unstable environments."],["Antarctic fossils","In 1969, Lystrosaurus fossils were found in Antarctica, matching those in South Africa and India. This helped confirm that these lands were once connected in Gondwana, part of Pangaea."],["Hibernation?","A 2020 study of Lystrosaurus tusks from Antarctica found growth rings suggesting it may have entered a hibernation-like state during the long, dark polar winters."],["Dicynodonts","Dicynodonts were a successful group of plant-eaters with beaks and tusks. Some later species, such as Lisowicia from Poland, grew as large as elephants."]],
k:[["Lived","About 255–250 million years ago"],["Group","Dicynodont synapsid"],["Size","About 0.6–2.5 meters long"],["Diet","Plants"],["Famous for","Surviving the end-Permian extinction"],["Found in","Africa, India, China, Russia, Antarctica"]],
d:[["1870","Lystrosaurus named"],["1969","Fossils found in Antarctica"],["2020","Study suggests hibernation-like behavior"]],
s2:[["Name","Lystrosaurus means ‘shovel lizard’."],["Not a dinosaur","Lystrosaurus lived before the dinosaurs and was more closely related to mammals than to dinosaurs."]],
g:[["Dicynodont","A beaked, tusked plant-eating synapsid"],["Mass extinction","When huge numbers of species die out quickly"],["Continental drift","The slow movement of continents"],["Gondwana","The southern part of Pangaea"]],
f:["Lystrosaurus survived the end-Permian extinction.","Lystrosaurus fossils were found in Antarctica.","Lystrosaurus was a plant-eater."],
fun:"For a while after the great extinction, Lystrosaurus may have been one of the most common land animals on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"gastornis",t:"Gastornis",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Gastornis",r:["terrorbirds","birds","dinobirds","fossils","moa","iceagemammals"],
p:[["A giant flightless bird","About 2 meters tall with a huge beak."],["After the dinosaurs","It lived 56 to 45 million years ago."],["Plant-eater","Once thought to be a predator, it probably ate plants."]],
l:"Gastornis was a giant flightless bird that lived about 56 to 45 million years ago, in the Paleocene and Eocene epochs, in what are now Europe, North America and Asia. It stood about 1.75 to 2 meters tall and may have weighed 100 to 175 kilograms, with tiny wings, strong legs and an enormous, deep beak. For many years it was pictured as a fierce predator that hunted small horses and other mammals, because of its huge head and beak. In North America it was called Diatryma, a name now considered the same animal. Studies in the 2010s and 2020s changed this view: its beak lacked a sharp hook for tearing flesh, its feet lacked big, curved talons, and chemical analysis of its bones suggested a plant-based diet. Scientists now think Gastornis used its massive beak to crack tough nuts, seeds and fibrous plants. Fossil eggshell fragments and possible footprints have also been found. Gastornis was related to ducks and geese, not to the predatory terror birds of South America, with which it was often compared.",
s:[["Discovery","Gastornis was named in 1855 by the French scientist Edmond Hébert, after Gaston Planté, who found the first bones near Paris. Planté later invented the rechargeable lead-acid battery."],["Predator or plant-eater?","A 2014 study of calcium isotopes in its bones and studies of its beak shape support a plant-eating diet. Large, beaked birds such as cassowaries and some parrots also crack hard foods."],["Footprints","Large bird footprints from Washington State, once thought to belong to Gastornis, show no sign of sharp talons, also supporting the idea that it was not a predator."],["Warm world","Gastornis lived during a very warm period, when forests covered much of the globe, even near the poles; its fossils have been found on Ellesmere Island in the Canadian Arctic."]],
k:[["Lived","About 56–45 million years ago"],["Where","Europe, North America, Asia"],["Height","About 1.75–2 meters"],["Diet","Probably plants, nuts and seeds"],["Relatives","Ducks and geese"],["Also called","Diatryma (North America)"]],
d:[["1855","Gastornis named in France"],["1876","Diatryma named in North America"],["2014","Study suggests a plant-based diet"]],
s2:[["Battery connection","The man who found the first Gastornis bones, Gaston Planté, went on to invent the rechargeable battery."],["Eggs","Pieces of eggshell possibly from Gastornis suggest its eggs were about 24 centimeters long."]],
g:[["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Eocene","The epoch from about 56 to 34 million years ago"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different mass"],["Talon","A sharp hooked claw"]],
f:["Gastornis could not fly.","Gastornis probably ate plants.","Gastornis was related to ducks and geese."],
fun:"Gastornis was once pictured hunting tiny horses, but it probably cracked nuts instead."});

ADDART({id:"moa",t:"Moa and Elephant Birds",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪶",w:"Moa",r:["nzcountry","madag","birds","dodo","extinction","gastornis"],
p:[["Giant island birds","Huge flightless birds of New Zealand and Madagascar."],["Taller than people","The largest moa reached about 3.6 meters with neck stretched."],["Lost to humans","Both died out after people arrived."]],
l:"Moa and elephant birds were giant flightless birds that lived on islands until relatively recently. Moa lived only in New Zealand, where there were nine species, ranging from turkey-sized to the giant moa (Dinornis), whose females could reach about 3.6 meters tall with their necks stretched up and weigh about 230 kilograms. Moa had no wings at all, not even tiny ones, and were the main large plant-eaters in New Zealand, which had no land mammals except bats. Their only natural predator was Haast’s eagle, the largest eagle known. Elephant birds lived on Madagascar. The largest, Vorombe titan or Aepyornis maximus, stood about 3 meters tall and may have weighed 650 to 860 kilograms, making it one of the heaviest birds ever. Elephant birds laid the largest eggs known, about 30 centimeters long, with a volume of about 150 chicken eggs. Both groups belong to the ratites, which include ostriches, emus, cassowaries and kiwi. Moa became extinct within about 200 years of Polynesian people settling New Zealand around 1300, and elephant birds by about 1,000 years ago, mainly due to hunting, egg collecting and habitat change.",
s:[["Moa","Moa were hunted by the ancestors of the Māori, who arrived in New Zealand around 1300 CE. Large numbers of moa bones have been found at ancient cooking sites. By around 1450, moa were extinct."],["Haast’s eagle","Haast’s eagle, which weighed up to about 15 kilograms, preyed on moa, attacking them with huge talons. It died out soon after the moa disappeared."],["Elephant birds","Elephant birds lived in Madagascar’s forests and grasslands. Their giant eggs are still found, sometimes complete. Studies of DNA show their closest living relatives are the small kiwi of New Zealand."],["Ratites","Ratites are flightless birds with flat breastbones. DNA studies show that their ancestors could fly and spread around the world, then lost flight several times separately."]],
k:[["Moa lived in","New Zealand"],["Elephant birds lived in","Madagascar"],["Tallest moa","About 3.6 m (neck stretched)"],["Heaviest elephant bird","About 650–860 kg"],["Largest egg","Elephant bird, about 30 cm"],["Relatives","Ostriches, emus, kiwi"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Elephant birds become extinct"],["c. 1300","Polynesian settlers reach New Zealand"],["c. 1450","Moa become extinct"],["1839","Richard Owen identifies moa from a bone"],["2018","Vorombe titan named"]],
s2:[["Kiwi’s cousin","The elephant bird’s closest living relative is the kiwi, despite living on opposite sides of the Indian Ocean."],["Sindbad’s roc","Elephant birds may have inspired legends of the roc, a giant bird in stories such as those of Sindbad the Sailor."]],
g:[["Ratite","A group of flightless birds with flat breastbones"],["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Extinct","No longer living anywhere"],["Māori","The Indigenous Polynesian people of New Zealand"]],
f:["Moa lived in New Zealand.","Elephant birds laid the largest known eggs.","Both became extinct after people arrived."],
fun:"One elephant bird egg could hold as much as about 150 chicken eggs."});

ADDART({id:"viscosity",t:"Viscosity",c:"Science",i:"🍯",w:"Viscosity",r:["fluids","surfacetension","temperature","volc","friction","atom"],
p:[["How thick a liquid is","Viscosity is resistance to flowing."],["Honey versus water","Honey is far more viscous than water."],["Changes with heat","Most liquids flow more easily when warm."]],
l:"Viscosity is a measure of how much a fluid, a liquid or a gas, resists flowing. A thick, slow-flowing liquid such as honey, syrup or tar has high viscosity, while a thin, runny liquid such as water or alcohol has low viscosity. Viscosity is like internal friction: as layers of a fluid slide past each other, the molecules pull on one another and slow the flow. Larger, tangled molecules, as in oils and syrups, generally make a liquid more viscous. Temperature has a big effect: most liquids become less viscous as they warm up, which is why honey pours more easily when heated and engine oil flows more slowly on a cold morning. Gases are the opposite and become slightly more viscous when hot. Viscosity matters in many areas: engineers choose oils with the right viscosity to protect engines, volcanologists know that runny basalt lava flows far while sticky, viscous lava can trap gas and cause explosive eruptions, and the viscosity of blood affects how easily the heart pumps it. Some unusual fluids, called non-Newtonian fluids, change viscosity when pushed or stirred, such as ketchup and a mixture of cornstarch and water.",
s:[["Measuring viscosity","Viscosity can be measured by timing how long a liquid takes to flow through a narrow tube, or how fast a ball sinks through it. Honey is roughly thousands of times more viscous than water at room temperature."],["The pitch drop experiment","In 1927, Professor Thomas Parnell at the University of Queensland in Australia poured heated pitch, a tar-like substance, into a funnel. It is so viscous that only nine drops fell in about 90 years, making it one of the longest-running experiments in the world."],["Non-Newtonian fluids","Ketchup becomes runnier when shaken. A cornstarch-and-water mixture, sometimes called oobleck, flows like a liquid when poured slowly but becomes almost solid when hit, so people can even run across a pool of it."],["Volcanoes","Low-viscosity basalt lava, as in Hawaii and Iceland, flows in rivers. High-viscosity lava, rich in silica, traps gas, which can lead to violent explosions such as at Mount St Helens in 1980."]],
k:[["Definition","Resistance to flowing"],["High viscosity","Honey, syrup, tar"],["Low viscosity","Water, alcohol"],["Effect of heat on liquids","Lowers viscosity"],["Strange fluids","Non-Newtonian (ketchup, oobleck)"],["Famous experiment","Pitch drop (since 1927)"]],
d:[["1687","Isaac Newton describes fluid friction"],["1927","Pitch drop experiment begins"],["2014","Ninth drop falls in the pitch drop experiment"]],
s2:[["Glass myth","Old windows are sometimes thicker at the bottom, leading to a myth that glass slowly flows. In fact, glass at room temperature flows far too slowly; uneven thickness came from how it was made."],["Ants in honey","Tiny insects can get trapped in viscous liquids like honey because they cannot push through quickly enough."]],
g:[["Viscosity","A fluid’s resistance to flow"],["Fluid","A liquid or gas"],["Non-Newtonian fluid","A fluid whose viscosity changes with force"],["Pitch","A very thick, tar-like substance"]],
f:["Honey has high viscosity.","Warm liquids usually flow more easily.","Ketchup is a non-Newtonian fluid."],
fun:"In the pitch drop experiment, only nine drops fell in about 90 years."});

ADDART({id:"centripetalforce",t:"Circular Motion and Centripetal Force",c:"Science",i:"🎡",w:"Centripetal force",r:["newton","grav","momentum","orbits","pendulums","satellites"],
p:[["Moving in circles","Anything going in a circle needs a force toward the center."],["Not flung outward","The ‘outward pull’ you feel is really inertia."],["Orbits and roller coasters","Gravity and tracks provide the force."]],
l:"When an object moves in a circle, it is constantly changing direction, even if its speed stays the same. According to Isaac Newton’s laws of motion, a change in direction requires a force, so anything moving in a circle must be pulled toward the center of the circle. This inward force is called centripetal force, from Latin words meaning ‘center-seeking’. Centripetal force is not a special kind of force but a role played by other forces: when you swing a ball on a string, the tension in the string pulls the ball inward; when a car turns a corner, friction between the tires and road provides it; and for the Moon orbiting Earth, or Earth orbiting the Sun, gravity provides it. If the force disappears, the object flies off in a straight line, tangent to the circle, as when a string breaks. The feeling of being pushed outward on a merry-go-round or in a turning car, often called centrifugal force, is really your body’s inertia, its tendency to keep moving in a straight line. The faster an object moves or the tighter the circle, the stronger the centripetal force needed.",
s:[["Orbits","A satellite in orbit is constantly falling toward Earth, but moving sideways so fast that it keeps missing. Gravity supplies the centripetal force. Low orbits require speeds of about 7.8 kilometers per second."],["Roller coaster loops","At the top of a loop, the track and gravity both push riders toward the center of the loop. If the coaster is fast enough, riders feel pressed into their seats instead of falling."],["Banked roads and tracks","Racetracks and highway curves are often tilted, or banked, so that part of the force from the road pushes cars toward the center of the turn, helping them corner safely at speed."],["Centrifuges","Centrifuges spin samples very fast. Denser materials move outward, separating blood cells from plasma or cream from milk. Large centrifuges also train astronauts and pilots for high g-forces."]],
k:[["Centripetal means","Center-seeking"],["Needed for","Any motion in a circle"],["Provided by","Tension, friction, gravity and others"],["Without it","Object moves in a straight line"],["‘Centrifugal force’","The effect of inertia"],["Stronger when","Faster speed or tighter circle"]],
d:[["1659","Christiaan Huygens works out the law of centrifugal force"],["1687","Newton explains orbits using gravity"],["1957","Sputnik 1 orbits Earth"]],
s2:[["Washing machines","The spin cycle of a washing machine flings water out through holes in the drum, because the clothes are held in by the drum but the water is not."],["Space stations of the future","Designers have proposed rotating space stations that create artificial gravity using circular motion."]],
g:[["Centripetal force","An inward force keeping something moving in a circle"],["Inertia","An object’s tendency to keep moving the same way"],["Orbit","A curved path around a planet or star"],["Centrifuge","A machine that spins things very fast"]],
f:["Circular motion needs an inward force.","Gravity keeps the Moon in orbit.","The outward feeling is caused by inertia."],
fun:"A satellite in orbit is always falling toward Earth but moving sideways fast enough to keep missing it."});

ADDART({id:"speedofsound",t:"The Speed of Sound",c:"Science",i:"🔊",w:"Speed of sound",r:["sound","dopplereffect","temperature","flight","thunderstorms","supersonic"],
p:[["About 343 meters per second","Sound’s speed in air at room temperature."],["Faster in water and steel","Sound travels faster in denser materials."],["Breaking the sound barrier","Supersonic jets fly faster than sound."]],
l:"The speed of sound is how fast sound waves travel through a material. In dry air at 20 °C, sound travels at about 343 meters per second, about 1,235 kilometers per hour, which is roughly a kilometer every three seconds. Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle, so it needs a material to travel through and cannot cross the vacuum of space. It moves faster in liquids and solids, where particles are packed closely and pushed back strongly: about 1,480 meters per second in water and about 5,000 to 6,000 meters per second in steel. In air, sound travels faster when it is warmer and slower when it is colder; that is why sound is slower high in the atmosphere where airplanes fly. Because light travels almost a million times faster than sound, you see lightning before you hear thunder; counting the seconds between them and dividing by three gives the distance of the storm in kilometers. Speeds compared with the speed of sound are called Mach numbers. In 1947, the American pilot Chuck Yeager became the first person to fly faster than sound, breaking the ‘sound barrier’ in the Bell X-1.",
s:[["Mach numbers","Mach 1 is the speed of sound. Mach 2 is twice as fast. The term honors the Austrian physicist Ernst Mach, who studied shock waves. Concorde, the supersonic airliner, cruised at about Mach 2 from 1976 to 2003."],["Sonic booms","When an object flies faster than sound, it pushes air into a cone-shaped shock wave. On the ground, people hear this as a loud boom. A cracking whip makes a tiny sonic boom because its tip moves faster than sound."],["Measuring distance","Bats, dolphins and ships use echoes to find objects, because the time sound takes to bounce back tells the distance. Ships use sonar to measure ocean depth."],["Quieter supersonic flight","NASA’s X-59 experimental aircraft, which first flew in 2025, is designed to fly faster than sound while making a softer ‘thump’ instead of a loud boom."]],
k:[["In air (20 °C)","About 343 m/s"],["In water","About 1,480 m/s"],["In steel","About 5,000–6,000 m/s"],["In space","Sound cannot travel"],["Faster than sound","Supersonic (Mach 1+)"],["First supersonic flight","1947, Chuck Yeager"]],
d:[["1640s","Marin Mersenne estimates the speed of sound"],["1947","Chuck Yeager breaks the sound barrier"],["1976","Concorde enters service"],["1997","Thrust SSC sets a supersonic land speed record"],["2025","NASA’s quiet supersonic X-59 first flies"]],
s2:[["Thunder counting","Count the seconds between a lightning flash and thunder, then divide by three to estimate how many kilometers away the storm is."],["Supersonic car","In 1997, the jet-powered car Thrust SSC became the first land vehicle to break the sound barrier, reaching about 1,228 km/h."]],
g:[["Speed of sound","How fast sound travels through a material"],["Mach number","Speed compared with the speed of sound"],["Supersonic","Faster than sound"],["Sonic boom","The loud noise from a supersonic shock wave"]],
f:["Sound travels about 343 m/s in air.","Sound travels faster in water than in air.","Sound cannot travel through space."],
fun:"The crack of a whip is a tiny sonic boom."});

ADDART({id:"pythagoras",t:"The Pythagorean Theorem",c:"Mathematics",i:"📐",w:"Pythagorean theorem",r:["euclid","shapes","greece","tessellations","logarithms"],
p:[["a² + b² = c²","The famous rule for right triangles."],["Ancient knowledge","Known in Babylon, India and China before Pythagoras."],["Everyday use","Builders, maps and screens use it."]],
l:"The Pythagorean theorem is one of the most famous rules in mathematics. It says that in a right triangle, a triangle with one 90-degree angle, the square of the longest side, called the hypotenuse, equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides. This is usually written a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. For example, a triangle with sides of 3 and 4 units has a hypotenuse of 5, because 9 + 16 = 25. The theorem is named after the ancient Greek thinker Pythagoras, who lived around 570 to 495 BCE, though it is not known whether he or his followers first proved it. The relationship was known much earlier: a Babylonian clay tablet called Plimpton 322, about 3,800 years old, lists sets of numbers that fit it, and it appears in ancient Indian and Chinese mathematics. The theorem has hundreds of known proofs, including one by a future US president, James Garfield. It is used in construction, navigation, surveying, computer graphics and physics, wherever people need to calculate distances, and it is the basis for measuring distance on coordinate grids.",
s:[["Pythagorean triples","Sets of whole numbers that fit the theorem, such as 3, 4, 5 and 5, 12, 13, are called Pythagorean triples. Builders have long used a rope with knots marking 3, 4 and 5 units to make perfect right angles."],["Ancient roots","The Babylonian tablet Plimpton 322 lists such triples. The Indian Baudhayana Sulba Sutra, perhaps from around 800 BCE, describes the relationship, and the Chinese text Zhoubi Suanjing includes a diagram proof."],["Many proofs","A collection published in 1927 contained more than 360 proofs. In 2023, two high school students in the United States presented a new proof using trigonometry, which was later published."],["Distances","On a map grid, the straight-line distance between two points can be found by treating the horizontal and vertical differences as the two shorter sides of a right triangle."]],
k:[["Formula","a² + b² = c²"],["Applies to","Right triangles"],["Hypotenuse","The longest side, opposite the right angle"],["Named after","Pythagoras (c. 570–495 BCE)"],["Known earlier in","Babylon, India, China"],["Simple example","3, 4, 5"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BCE","Babylonian tablet Plimpton 322"],["c. 530 BCE","Pythagoras teaches in southern Italy"],["c. 300 BCE","Euclid includes a proof in his Elements"],["1876","James Garfield publishes a proof"]],
s2:[["Screen sizes","TV and phone screens are measured diagonally; the diagonal can be found from the width and height using the theorem."],["Three dimensions","The theorem extends to 3D: the diagonal of a box is found using all three dimensions."]],
g:[["Right triangle","A triangle with a 90-degree angle"],["Hypotenuse","The longest side of a right triangle"],["Square (of a number)","A number multiplied by itself"],["Proof","A logical argument showing something is true"]],
f:["The Pythagorean theorem is a² + b² = c².","It works for right triangles.","A 3-4-5 triangle is a right triangle."],
fun:"US President James Garfield published his own proof of the Pythagorean theorem."});

ADDART({id:"romannumerals",t:"Roman Numerals",c:"Mathematics",i:"🏛️",w:"Roman numerals",r:["rome","zero","binary","clocks","calendars","latin","fib"],
p:[["Letters as numbers","I, V, X, L, C, D and M."],["Adding and subtracting","IV is 4, VI is 6."],["Still in use","On clocks, monarchs’ names and film credits."]],
l:"Roman numerals are a number system that originated in ancient Rome and was used throughout Europe for well over a thousand years. It uses seven letters with set values: I = 1, V = 5, X = 10, L = 50, C = 100, D = 500 and M = 1,000. Numbers are generally written from largest to smallest and added together: VIII is 5 + 3 = 8, and MDCLXVI is 1,666. To avoid writing four of the same symbol in a row, a smaller numeral placed before a larger one is subtracted: IV is 4, IX is 9, XL is 40, XC is 90, CD is 400 and CM is 900. So 2026 is written MMXXVI. The Roman system has no symbol for zero and is not a place-value system, which makes arithmetic such as multiplication and division difficult; Romans did calculations on counting boards and abacuses instead. From the late Middle Ages, Europe gradually adopted Hindu–Arabic numerals, the 0 to 9 digits used today, which are far easier to calculate with. Roman numerals are still used on clock faces, for the names of monarchs and popes (such as Elizabeth II), for numbering book chapters, for Olympic Games and Super Bowls, and for copyright dates in films.",
s:[["Origins","Roman numerals probably developed from tally marks used by shepherds and traders, with V possibly representing an open hand and X two hands or a crossed tally."],["Clocks","Many clocks with Roman numerals show 4 as IIII rather than IV. One explanation is that IIII balances the VIII on the opposite side of the dial."],["Large numbers","For numbers above 3,999, a bar over a numeral was sometimes used to multiply it by 1,000, so V with a bar meant 5,000."],["The switch to Arabic numerals","Fibonacci’s book Liber Abaci (1202) helped introduce Hindu–Arabic numerals to Europe. Merchants and bankers adopted them because they made calculations much easier."]],
k:[["Symbols","I, V, X, L, C, D, M"],["Values","1, 5, 10, 50, 100, 500, 1,000"],["Subtraction examples","IV = 4, IX = 9, XC = 90"],["2026","MMXXVI"],["Missing","A symbol for zero"],["Still used","Clocks, monarchs, events, chapters"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Roman numerals in use in Rome"],["1202","Fibonacci promotes Hindu–Arabic numerals in Europe"],["1500s","Arabic numerals widely used in Europe"]],
s2:[["Super Bowl","The American football championship, the Super Bowl, has used Roman numerals since 1971; Super Bowl LX (60) was played in 2026."],["Longest year","Between years 1 and 3999, MMMDCCCLXXXVIII (3888) has the longest Roman numeral."]],
g:[["Numeral","A symbol for a number"],["Place value","A digit’s value depending on its position"],["Hindu–Arabic numerals","The digits 0–9"],["Abacus","A counting frame with beads"]],
f:["X stands for 10 in Roman numerals.","Roman numerals have no zero.","IV means 4."],
fun:"Many clocks show 4 as IIII instead of IV."});

ADDART({id:"magicsquares",t:"Magic Squares",c:"Mathematics",i:"🔢",w:"Magic square",r:["prime","fib","pythagoras","romannumerals","tessellations","gametheory"],
p:[["Number grids","Every row, column and diagonal adds to the same total."],["Ancient puzzle","The Lo Shu square from China is thousands of years old."],["In art","Albrecht Dürer put one in a famous engraving."]],
l:"A magic square is a square grid filled with numbers so that the numbers in every row, every column and both main diagonals add up to the same total, called the magic constant. The smallest true example uses the numbers 1 to 9 in a 3 by 3 grid, where every line adds up to 15. This square, known in China as the Lo Shu, appears in an ancient legend about a turtle that emerged from the Luo River with the pattern of dots on its shell, and it has been connected with Chinese philosophy and feng shui. Magic squares spread to India, the Islamic world and Europe, where they were studied by mathematicians and sometimes engraved on amulets as lucky charms. A famous 4 by 4 magic square appears in the 1514 engraving Melencolia I by the German artist Albrecht Dürer; its bottom row includes the numbers 15 and 14, giving the year the work was made, and the numbers in many other patterns also add up to the magic constant 34. For a square using the numbers 1 to n², the magic constant is n(n² + 1)/2. Magic squares are related to other puzzles, such as Sudoku, and to areas of mathematics such as combinatorics.",
s:[["The Lo Shu","The 3 by 3 Lo Shu square has 5 at the center, even numbers in the corners and odd numbers on the edges. There are eight ways to write a 3 by 3 magic square with 1 to 9, but they are all rotations or reflections of the Lo Shu."],["Dürer’s square","Dürer’s square has the magic constant 34. The four corners, the four central squares and each 2 by 2 quarter also add to 34."],["How many?","There are 880 different 4 by 4 magic squares using 1 to 16, not counting rotations and reflections, and about 275 million 5 by 5 squares. The number of 6 by 6 squares is still not exactly known."],["Making them","Simple methods exist for building odd-sized magic squares, such as the ‘Siamese method’, which places consecutive numbers diagonally up and to the right, wrapping around the edges."]],
k:[["Rule","Rows, columns and diagonals have the same sum"],["Smallest example","3 × 3, using 1–9"],["3 × 3 magic constant","15"],["4 × 4 magic constant","34"],["Ancient example","Lo Shu (China)"],["Famous in art","Dürer’s Melencolia I (1514)"]],
d:[["c. 650 BCE","Lo Shu square described in China (legend older)"],["c. 900","Magic squares studied in the Islamic world"],["1514","Dürer’s Melencolia I"],["1693","All 880 4 × 4 squares counted by Bernard Frénicle de Bessy"]],
s2:[["Sagrada Família","A 4 by 4 square on the Sagrada Família in Barcelona has a magic constant of 33, but it repeats some numbers."],["Benjamin Franklin","Benjamin Franklin made magic squares to pass the time during boring meetings."]],
g:[["Magic square","A grid where all lines have the same sum"],["Magic constant","The sum of each row, column and diagonal"],["Engraving","A picture printed from carved metal"],["Combinatorics","The math of counting and arranging"]],
f:["In a magic square, rows and columns add to the same number.","The Lo Shu square comes from China.","Dürer’s square adds to 34."],
fun:"Benjamin Franklin made magic squares to keep himself busy in boring meetings."});

ADDART({id:"antibioticresistance",t:"Antibiotic Resistance",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🦠",w:"Antimicrobial resistance",r:["antibiotics","bacteria","vacc","who","evolution","pandemic"],
p:[["Superbugs","Bacteria that antibiotics can no longer kill."],["One in six infections","WHO found widespread resistance in 2025."],["Use them wisely","Overuse speeds up resistance."]],
l:"Antibiotic resistance happens when bacteria change so that antibiotics, the medicines used to kill them or stop their growth, no longer work against them. Infections caused by resistant bacteria, sometimes called superbugs, are harder to treat, last longer and are more likely to cause serious illness or death. Resistance is a natural result of evolution: when antibiotics are used, bacteria that happen to have genes allowing them to survive multiply, while others die. Bacteria can also share resistance genes with each other. Overuse and misuse of antibiotics speed this up, such as taking them for viral infections like colds and flu, against which they do not work, not finishing a prescribed course when told to, or using them to make farm animals grow faster. Antibiotic resistance is part of a wider problem called antimicrobial resistance (AMR), which also includes resistance in viruses, fungi and parasites. AMR was directly responsible for more than a million deaths a year in recent estimates. In October 2025, the World Health Organization reported that about one in six laboratory-confirmed bacterial infections worldwide in 2023 were resistant to antibiotic treatments.",
s:[["How resistance spreads","Resistant bacteria spread between people, through hospitals, food, water and travel. Genes for resistance can move between bacteria on small DNA loops called plasmids."],["Superbugs","Well-known resistant bacteria include methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) and strains of E. coli, Klebsiella and the bacterium that causes tuberculosis that resist many drugs."],["What people can do","Use antibiotics only when prescribed by a health professional, take them exactly as directed, never share them, and prevent infections through handwashing, vaccination and safe food handling."],["New medicines","Few new classes of antibiotics have been discovered since the 1980s, partly because they earn little money for drug companies. Scientists are exploring new sources, such as soil microbes, viruses that attack bacteria called phages, and artificial intelligence to design new drugs."]],
k:[["Cause","Bacteria evolving to survive antibiotics"],["Sped up by","Overuse and misuse of antibiotics"],["Famous superbug","MRSA"],["Resistant infections (WHO, 2023 data)","About one in six"],["Wider problem","Antimicrobial resistance (AMR)"],["Prevention","Careful use, hygiene, vaccines"]],
d:[["1928","Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin"],["1945","Fleming warns about resistance in his Nobel lecture"],["1961","MRSA first identified"],["2015","WHO Global Action Plan on AMR"],["2025","WHO reports one in six infections resistant"]],
s2:[["Fleming’s warning","In his 1945 Nobel Prize speech, Alexander Fleming warned that misuse of penicillin could lead to resistant bacteria."],["World AMR Awareness Week","The WHO holds World AMR Awareness Week every November."]],
g:[["Antibiotic","A medicine that kills or stops bacteria"],["Resistance","The ability to survive a drug"],["Superbug","A bacterium resistant to many antibiotics"],["Plasmid","A small loop of DNA in bacteria"]],
f:["Antibiotics do not work against viruses.","Overuse speeds up antibiotic resistance.","MRSA is a resistant bacterium."],
fun:"Alexander Fleming warned about antibiotic resistance as early as 1945."});

ADDART({id:"ultraprocessed",t:"Ultra-Processed Foods",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🍟",w:"Ultra-processed food",r:["nutri","fastfood","obesity","diabetes","plantbased","foodwaste"],
p:[["Made in factories","Foods built from industrial ingredients and additives."],["A big part of diets","Over half of calories in some countries."],["Health concerns","Linked to obesity, diabetes and heart disease."]],
l:"Ultra-processed foods are industrially made foods and drinks that are created mostly from substances extracted from foods, such as oils, starches, sugars and proteins, combined with additives like flavorings, colorings, emulsifiers and sweeteners, often using processes not used in home cooking. Examples include soft drinks, packaged snacks, sweets, many breakfast cereals, instant noodles, reconstituted meat products such as chicken nuggets and hot dogs, and many ready meals. The term comes from the NOVA classification, developed in 2009 by Brazilian researchers led by Carlos Monteiro, which groups foods by how much and why they are processed: unprocessed or minimally processed foods, culinary ingredients, processed foods and ultra-processed foods. Ultra-processed foods are usually cheap, convenient, long-lasting and designed to be very tasty, and they make up more than half of calories eaten in countries such as the United States and the United Kingdom, and a rapidly growing share elsewhere. Many studies link diets high in ultra-processed foods to obesity, type 2 diabetes, heart disease and other health problems. A major series of papers in The Lancet in November 2025 called for policies to reduce them, though scientists still debate how much processing itself, rather than ingredients such as sugar, salt and fat, causes harm.",
s:[["The NOVA groups","Group 1: unprocessed or minimally processed foods, such as fruit, eggs and milk. Group 2: culinary ingredients, such as oil, butter and salt. Group 3: processed foods, such as cheese, canned vegetables and fresh bread. Group 4: ultra-processed foods."],["Evidence","A 2019 US experiment by Kevin Hall found that people offered ultra-processed diets ate about 500 more calories a day than when offered minimally processed diets with similar nutrients. Many observational studies link high intake to poorer health."],["Debates","Some scientists argue that the category is too broad, lumping together foods such as sugary drinks and wholegrain bread, and that more research is needed on why ultra-processed foods might be harmful."],["Policies","Some countries, such as Brazil, advise people in national dietary guidelines to avoid ultra-processed foods. Others use warning labels, sugar taxes and limits on marketing to children."]],
k:[["Definition","Industrial foods made mainly from extracted substances and additives"],["Classification","NOVA (Brazil, 2009)"],["Examples","Soft drinks, packaged snacks, nuggets, instant noodles"],["Share of calories","Over half in the US and UK"],["Health links","Obesity, diabetes, heart disease"],["Lancet series","November 2025"]],
d:[["2009","NOVA classification proposed"],["2014","Brazil’s dietary guidelines advise avoiding ultra-processed foods"],["2019","Controlled feeding study shows people overeat ultra-processed diets"],["2025","Lancet series on ultra-processed foods"]],
s2:[["Reading labels","A long ingredient list with items you would not find in a home kitchen, such as emulsifiers or flavor enhancers, often signals an ultra-processed food."],["Not all processing","Freezing, canning and fermenting are forms of processing too, but foods like frozen peas or yogurt are not considered ultra-processed."]],
g:[["Ultra-processed food","A food made industrially from extracted ingredients and additives"],["Additive","A substance added to food for flavor, color or texture"],["Emulsifier","An additive that helps oil and water mix"],["NOVA","A system grouping foods by processing"]],
f:["The NOVA system classifies foods by processing.","Soft drinks are ultra-processed foods.","Ultra-processed foods are linked to health problems."],
fun:"Brazil’s national dietary guidelines simply advise people to avoid ultra-processed foods."});

ADDART({id:"digitalnomads",t:"Digital Nomads",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Digital nomad",r:["remotework","gigeconomy","overtourism","internet","creatoreconomy","migration"],
p:[["Working from anywhere","People who travel while working online."],["Special visas","Many countries offer digital nomad visas."],["Effects on cities","Popular spots see rising rents and new cafés."]],
l:"Digital nomads are people who work remotely using the internet while traveling or living in different places, rather than staying in one home or office. Many work as freelancers, programmers, designers, writers, marketers or online teachers, or are employees whose companies allow remote work. With a laptop, phone and good internet connection, they may work from cafés, shared coworking spaces or rented apartments, moving between countries every few weeks or months. The lifestyle grew in the 2010s as fast internet spread and accelerated greatly after the COVID-19 pandemic, when many companies allowed employees to work from home. To attract them, dozens of countries, including Estonia, Portugal, Spain, Croatia, Costa Rica, Indonesia, Thailand and Brazil, have introduced special ‘digital nomad visas’ letting remote workers stay legally for a year or more while working for foreign employers. Popular destinations include Lisbon, Bali, Chiang Mai, Mexico City and Medellín. Digital nomads can bring money to local businesses, but in some cities residents complain that they push up rents and change neighborhoods.",
s:[["Visas","Estonia launched one of the first digital nomad visas in 2020. Most require proof of a minimum income from work done for clients or employers outside the country, and health insurance."],["Coworking and coliving","Coworking spaces rent desks to remote workers, and coliving houses combine rooms with shared workspaces and social events."],["Challenges","Digital nomads face time-zone differences, unreliable internet, loneliness, complicated tax rules and the stress of constant travel. Many settle into ‘slow travel’, staying months in one place."],["Local effects","In cities such as Lisbon and Mexico City, rising numbers of foreign remote workers and short-term rentals have been linked to higher rents, leading to protests and debates about regulation."]],
k:[["Definition","People who work online while traveling"],["Typical jobs","Programming, design, writing, marketing, teaching"],["Boosted by","Fast internet and the COVID-19 pandemic"],["Early visa","Estonia (2020)"],["Popular places","Lisbon, Bali, Chiang Mai, Mexico City"],["Concerns","Rising rents, tax rules, loneliness"]],
d:[["1997","The book Digital Nomad popularizes the term"],["2010s","Coworking spaces spread worldwide"],["2020","Estonia launches a digital nomad visa"],["2020–2022","Remote work surges during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Not new","Traveling workers have existed for centuries, such as traveling merchants and artisans, but the internet made it possible for office jobs."],["Time zones","Many digital nomads choose places in time zones close to their clients so meetings are at reasonable hours."]],
g:[["Digital nomad","A person who works online while traveling"],["Remote work","Working away from the employer’s office"],["Coworking space","A shared office for many workers"],["Visa","Official permission to stay in a country"]],
f:["Digital nomads work remotely while traveling.","Many countries offer digital nomad visas.","Estonia launched an early digital nomad visa."],
fun:"The term ‘digital nomad’ was popularized by a book published in 1997, long before most people had smartphones."});

ADDART({id:"shrinkflation",t:"Shrinkflation",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📦",w:"Shrinkflation",r:["inflation","consumerrights","money","fastfood","ultraprocessed"],
p:[["Same price, less product","Packages quietly shrink while prices stay the same."],["A hidden price rise","It raises the cost per gram or per item."],["Labels and laws","Some countries now require warnings."]],
l:"Shrinkflation is when companies reduce the size or quantity of a product, such as fewer grams of chocolate, fewer chips in a bag or fewer sheets on a toilet roll, while keeping the price the same or nearly the same. The word combines ‘shrink’ and ‘inflation’ and was popularized by the British economist Pippa Malmgren around 2009. Because the price on the shelf does not change, many shoppers do not notice, but the real price per gram, per liter or per item goes up. Companies say shrinkflation helps them deal with rising costs of ingredients, packaging, energy and transport without the shock of a higher price tag. Critics call it a sneaky way to raise prices. Shrinkflation became especially noticeable during the high inflation of 2021 to 2023. Related practices include ‘skimpflation’, when the quality of ingredients or service is reduced, such as replacing some cocoa butter with cheaper fats. To help consumers, many countries require shops to display unit prices, and some have introduced special rules: since July 2024, French supermarkets must put labels on products that have shrunk in size without a matching price cut.",
s:[["How to spot it","Checking the unit price, the cost per 100 grams or per liter, on shelf labels is the best way to compare products and notice shrinkflation. Changes to packaging, such as a deeper dent in the bottom of a jar, can hide smaller amounts."],["Famous examples","Chocolate bars with wider gaps between the peaks, cereal boxes that got thinner, and bags of chips with more air have all been cited as examples."],["Rules","France’s 2024 rule requires labels for shrunk products for two months. Other countries, including Brazil, South Korea and Hungary, have rules on notifying customers of size changes."],["Economics","Economists include product size in inflation measurements, so official inflation statistics already account for shrinkflation, even if shoppers miss it."]],
k:[["Meaning","Smaller product, same price"],["Word popularized","About 2009, Pippa Malmgren"],["Effect","Higher price per unit"],["Related term","Skimpflation (lower quality)"],["How to spot","Compare unit prices"],["French labeling rule","Since July 2024"]],
d:[["2009","The word shrinkflation is popularized"],["2021–2023","High inflation makes shrinkflation more common"],["2024","France requires shrinkflation labels"]],
s2:[["Toilet paper","Some rolls of toilet paper have gotten narrower or have fewer sheets, though they look about the same size."],["Not illegal","In most countries, shrinkflation is legal as long as the package shows the correct amount."]],
g:[["Shrinkflation","Reducing product size without lowering price"],["Inflation","General rise in prices"],["Unit price","Price per standard amount, such as per 100 g"],["Skimpflation","Reducing quality without lowering price"]],
f:["Shrinkflation means less product for the same price.","Unit prices help spot shrinkflation.","France requires labels for shrunk products."],
fun:"Some chocolate bars have kept their packaging size while the chocolate inside got smaller."});

ADDART({id:"lootboxes",t:"Loot Boxes and Microtransactions",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎁",w:"Loot box",r:["videogames","gambling","esports","creatoreconomy","gameconsoles","consumerrights"],
p:[["Paying inside games","Small purchases for items, skins or boosts."],["Mystery rewards","Loot boxes give random prizes."],["Gambling debates","Some countries treat loot boxes like gambling."]],
l:"Microtransactions are small purchases made inside video games, often with real money, for items such as character outfits called skins, weapons, extra lives, levels, in-game currency or season passes. They are common in free-to-play games, which earn most of their money this way, and many paid games also include them. A loot box is a kind of microtransaction in which players pay for a virtual box, card pack or spin that gives random items, some common and some rare. Similar systems in Japanese and Chinese mobile games are often called gacha, after capsule toy machines. Because players do not know what they will get, loot boxes resemble gambling, and critics worry that they can encourage overspending, especially among children and teenagers. Governments have responded in different ways: Belgium declared paid loot boxes illegal under its gambling laws in 2018, the Netherlands challenged some in court, China requires games to publish the odds of winning items, and other countries have asked companies to regulate themselves. Major app stores and console makers now require games to disclose loot box odds. Supporters say microtransactions let people play for free and support ongoing game development.",
s:[["How they make money","A small share of players, sometimes called ‘whales’, spend large amounts and provide much of a free-to-play game’s income. Games may use limited-time offers and special currencies to make spending feel less like real money."],["Cosmetic or pay-to-win","Many players accept cosmetic items that change only appearance, but dislike ‘pay-to-win’ purchases that give advantages over players who do not pay."],["Rules around the world","Belgium’s gambling commission ruled in 2018 that paid loot boxes are gambling. China has required odds disclosure since 2017. In 2022, the UK government chose not to ban them but called for industry action, and some games now require parental permission for purchases."],["Protecting children","Parents can use console and phone settings to limit or block in-game purchases. Some countries require refunds when children make purchases without permission."]],
k:[["Microtransaction","A small in-game purchase"],["Loot box","A paid mystery reward"],["Japanese style","Gacha"],["Ban","Belgium (2018)"],["Odds disclosure","Required in China (2017) and by major platforms"],["Concern","Gambling-like spending by young players"]],
d:[["2004","Early paid random items in online games"],["2017","China requires games to publish loot box odds"],["2018","Belgium rules paid loot boxes are gambling"],["2019","Major platforms require odds disclosure"]],
s2:[["Battle passes","Many games now sell ‘battle passes’, which unlock rewards as players progress, as an alternative to random loot boxes."],["Gacha","The word gacha comes from ‘gachapon’, Japanese toy vending machines that give random capsule toys."]],
g:[["Microtransaction","A small in-game purchase"],["Loot box","A paid virtual item with random contents"],["Free-to-play","A game that is free to download"],["Odds","The chance of something happening"]],
f:["Loot boxes give random rewards.","Belgium treats paid loot boxes as gambling.","Many platforms require loot box odds to be shown."],
fun:"The word ‘gacha’ comes from Japanese capsule-toy vending machines."});

ADDART({id:"righttorepair",t:"The Right to Repair",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🔧",w:"Right to repair",r:["ewaste","smartphone","consumerrights","recycling","fastfashion"],
p:[["Fixing what you own","The idea that people should be able to repair their things."],["Parts and manuals","Laws require makers to provide spare parts and information."],["Less waste","Repair keeps products in use longer."]],
l:"The right to repair is the idea that people and independent repair shops should be able to fix the products they own, such as phones, laptops, home appliances, cars and farm equipment, instead of having to rely only on the manufacturer or replace them. Supporters argue that many products are made hard to repair: they may be glued shut, use special screws, have parts that are paired by software so replacements do not work, or lack available spare parts, tools and repair manuals. This can make repairs expensive or impossible, forcing people to buy new products and adding to the growing amount of electronic waste. Manufacturers have sometimes argued that restrictions protect safety, security and intellectual property. Right-to-repair movements, supported by groups such as the repair website iFixit and consumer organizations, have won new laws in recent years. In the European Union, ecodesign rules since June 2025 require new smartphones and tablets to have replaceable batteries and parts available for years, and the EU Right to Repair Directive, which applied from 31 July 2026, obliges makers of many products to offer repairs at reasonable prices. Several US states, including New York, Minnesota and California, have passed repair laws too.",
s:[["Why it matters","Electronic waste is the fastest-growing kind of household waste. Repairing products saves money and resources and reduces the need to mine materials and manufacture new goods."],["Farm equipment","Farmers in the United States campaigned for the right to repair tractors that require manufacturer software to fix. Some companies agreed to provide more tools and information."],["Repair cafés","Repair cafés, started in Amsterdam in 2009, are community events where volunteers help people fix broken items for free. Thousands now run around the world."],["Repairability scores","France introduced a repairability index in 2021, requiring products such as phones and laptops to display a score from 1 to 10 showing how easy they are to repair. The EU added repairability labels for phones in 2025."]],
k:[["Idea","People can fix what they own"],["Main issues","Spare parts, tools, manuals, software locks"],["EU smartphone rules","Since June 2025"],["EU Right to Repair Directive","Applies from 31 July 2026"],["US state laws","New York, Minnesota, California and others"],["Benefits","Lower costs, less e-waste"]],
d:[["2009","First repair café in Amsterdam"],["2012","Massachusetts passes a car repair law"],["2021","France introduces a repairability index"],["2025","EU smartphone ecodesign rules take effect"],["2026","EU Right to Repair Directive applies"]],
s2:[["Repair manuals online","Websites share free repair guides for thousands of devices, written by volunteers."],["Batteries","Replaceable batteries can greatly extend the life of a phone, because batteries often wear out before other parts."]],
g:[["Right to repair","The ability to fix products you own"],["E-waste","Discarded electronic devices"],["Spare part","A replacement component"],["Repairability","How easy something is to fix"]],
f:["The right to repair helps people fix their products.","The EU requires replaceable smartphone parts.","Repair cafés began in Amsterdam."],
fun:"The first repair café, in Amsterdam in 2009, inspired thousands more around the world."});

ADDART({id:"choirs",t:"Choirs and Choral Singing",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎼",w:"Choir",r:["gospel","orchestra","voice","musicw","musicals","folkmusic"],
p:[["Singing together","A choir is a group of singers performing together."],["Voice parts","Soprano, alto, tenor and bass."],["Good for you","Group singing can lift mood and build friendships."]],
l:"A choir, or chorus, is a group of singers who perform together, usually with each person singing one of several parts. Choral singing is one of the oldest and most widespread forms of music-making, found in religious worship, schools, communities, concert halls and celebrations around the world. In a typical mixed choir, voices are divided into four main parts, from highest to lowest: soprano and alto, usually sung by women and children, and tenor and bass, usually sung by men, abbreviated SATB. Choirs may sing unaccompanied, called a cappella, or with an organ, piano or orchestra. A conductor leads the choir, keeping time and shaping the music. Choral music has a long history, from medieval plainchant sung by monks to great works by composers such as Johann Sebastian Bach, George Frideric Handel, whose oratorio Messiah (1742) is performed worldwide each year, Ludwig van Beethoven, whose Ninth Symphony ends with a choral ‘Ode to Joy’, and many modern composers. Traditions include South African choral singing, Welsh male voice choirs, gospel choirs and Estonia’s massive song festivals. Studies suggest group singing can improve mood, reduce stress and strengthen social bonds.",
s:[["Voice parts","Sopranos sing the highest notes and often the melody. Altos sing lower harmony. Tenors are the higher men’s voices, and basses the lowest. Some choirs add baritones and mezzo-sopranos."],["Messiah","Handel’s Messiah, first performed in Dublin in 1742, includes the famous ‘Hallelujah’ chorus. By tradition, audiences in many places stand during it."],["Song festivals","Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania hold huge song festivals where tens of thousands of singers perform together. In 1988–1991, singing gatherings in the Baltic states became part of the ‘Singing Revolution’ for independence."],["Health and community","Research suggests that singing in a choir can release feel-good chemicals, help breathing and give a strong sense of belonging. Community choirs for people with conditions such as Parkinson’s disease and lung disease have grown."]],
k:[["Definition","A group of singers performing together"],["Main parts","Soprano, alto, tenor, bass (SATB)"],["Without instruments","A cappella"],["Leader","Conductor"],["Famous work","Handel’s Messiah (1742)"],["Huge gatherings","Baltic song festivals"]],
d:[["c. 600s","Plainchant develops in churches"],["1742","Handel’s Messiah premieres in Dublin"],["1824","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony with chorus premieres"],["1869","First Estonian Song Festival"],["1988","Singing Revolution in the Baltic states"]],
s2:[["Virtual choirs","Composer Eric Whitacre created ‘virtual choirs’ that combine videos of thousands of singers recorded separately around the world."],["Barbershop","Barbershop music features close four-part harmony, usually sung a cappella by quartets."]],
g:[["Choir","A group of singers"],["A cappella","Singing without instruments"],["Conductor","The person who leads a choir or orchestra"],["Oratorio","A large musical work for choir, soloists and orchestra"]],
f:["Choirs often sing in four parts.","A cappella means without instruments.","Handel wrote Messiah."],
fun:"At Estonia’s song festivals, more than 20,000 singers can perform on one stage at the same time."});

ADDART({id:"mime",t:"Mime and Clowning",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🤡",w:"Mime artist",r:["asiantheatre","circus","comedy","puppetry","worlddance"],
p:[["Stories without words","Mime uses movement and gesture."],["Make people laugh","Clowns use physical comedy."],["Ancient roots","From Greek and Roman theater to the modern circus."]],
l:"Mime is the art of telling a story or expressing ideas through body movement, gesture and facial expression, without speech. A mime artist might pretend to be trapped in an invisible box, pull an imaginary rope or walk against the wind, making the audience ‘see’ objects that are not there. Clowning is a related art of physical comedy, in which performers use exaggerated movements, slapstick, playful mistakes and colorful costumes to make audiences laugh. Both have ancient roots. In ancient Greece and Rome, performers called mimes acted out comic scenes, and in the Italian commedia dell’arte of the 1500s to 1700s, masked stock characters such as Harlequin and Pierrot performed physical comedy. In 19th-century France, Jean-Gaspard Deburau made the sad, white-faced Pierrot famous, and in the 20th century, the French artist Marcel Marceau, with his character Bip, became the world’s best-known mime. Silent film stars such as Charlie Chaplin and Buster Keaton used mime and clowning on screen. Clowns perform in circuses, theaters and hospitals, where ‘clown doctors’ help cheer up sick children. Many cultures have their own clown traditions, from Native American sacred clowns to the jesters of medieval courts.",
s:[["Marcel Marceau","Marcel Marceau performed as Bip, a white-faced character in a striped shirt and battered hat, from 1947 until his death in 2007. During the Second World War, he helped Jewish children escape to safety, using mime to keep them quiet."],["Commedia dell’arte","Italian troupes improvised plays around stock characters, such as the clever servant Harlequin and the greedy merchant Pantalone. Their physical comedy influenced clowns, pantomime and comedy for centuries."],["Clown doctors","Programs such as the Big Apple Circus Clown Care, founded in 1986, and similar groups in many countries send trained clowns into hospitals to bring play and laughter to children."],["Types of clown","Circus traditions include the elegant ‘whiteface’ clown, the clumsy ‘auguste’ with a red nose, and the sad ‘tramp’ character made famous by Emmett Kelly."]],
k:[["Mime","Storytelling without words"],["Clowning","Physical comedy"],["Ancient roots","Greek and Roman mimes"],["Famous mime","Marcel Marceau (Bip)"],["Silent film stars","Charlie Chaplin, Buster Keaton"],["In hospitals","Clown doctors"]],
d:[["1500s","Commedia dell’arte flourishes in Italy"],["1819","Deburau begins performing Pierrot in Paris"],["1947","Marcel Marceau creates Bip"],["1986","Clown Care hospital program founded"]],
s2:[["Red nose","The red nose of the auguste clown has become a worldwide symbol of clowning."],["Invisible wall","The ‘invisible wall’ is one of mime’s most famous routines; the performer’s flat hands make the audience imagine a solid surface."]],
g:[["Mime","Acting without words"],["Slapstick","Comedy based on exaggerated physical action"],["Commedia dell’arte","Italian improvised comedy with masked characters"],["Gesture","A movement expressing meaning"]],
f:["Mime tells stories without words.","Marcel Marceau was a famous mime.","Clown doctors visit hospitals."],
fun:"During the Second World War, Marcel Marceau used mime to keep children quiet while helping them escape to safety."});

ADDART({id:"emulators",t:"Emulators and Game Preservation",c:"Technology",i:"🕹️",w:"Emulator",r:["videogames","gameconsoles","arcadegames","operatingsystems","coding","museums"],
p:[["Old machines in software","An emulator makes one computer act like another."],["Saving game history","Many old games would be lost without them."],["Legal questions","Copyright limits how games can be shared."]],
l:"An emulator is a program that allows one computer system to behave like another, so that software made for the original system can run on a different one. For example, an emulator on a modern computer can imitate an old video game console, arcade machine or home computer, letting people play games or run programs that would otherwise need the original hardware. Emulators work by recreating, in software, how the original machine’s processor, memory, graphics and sound chips behaved. They are used for many purposes: game companies use emulation to re-release classic games on new consoles; developers use emulators to test apps for phones; and businesses use them to keep running old software. Emulation is especially important for preserving video game history. Original cartridges, discs and consoles wear out, and many games were never re-released; a 2023 study by the Video Game History Foundation found that about 87 percent of classic games released in the United States were not commercially available. Museums and archives use emulation to preserve games. Emulators themselves are generally legal, but copying and sharing copyrighted games without permission is not.",
s:[["How emulators work","An emulator reads the original machine’s instructions and translates them into instructions the host computer can run, while imitating timing, graphics and sound. Accurate emulation of fast or unusual hardware can need a very powerful computer."],["Re-releases","Many ‘classic’ mini consoles and online game libraries from companies such as Nintendo, Sega and Sony use emulation to run old games on new hardware."],["Preservation","Organizations such as the Video Game History Foundation, the Internet Archive and national libraries work to preserve games, manuals and magazines. Some countries have legal exceptions allowing libraries to preserve software."],["Beyond games","Emulators let modern computers run old office programs and operating systems, and phone app developers test their apps on emulated devices before releasing them."]],
k:[["Definition","Software that imitates another computer system"],["Common use","Running old games on new devices"],["Industry use","Re-releases and app testing"],["Classic games unavailable","About 87% (2023 study, US)"],["Legal","Emulators yes; sharing copyrighted games no"],["Preservation groups","Video Game History Foundation, Internet Archive"]],
d:[["1960s","IBM uses emulation for compatibility between computers"],["1997","MAME arcade emulator project begins"],["2016","Nintendo releases the NES Classic Edition"],["2023","Study finds most classic games unavailable"]],
s2:[["Arcade history","The MAME project has documented thousands of arcade machines so their games and hardware are not forgotten."],["Speedrunners","Some speedrunners and researchers use emulators to study games frame by frame."]],
g:[["Emulator","Software that imitates another computer"],["Preservation","Keeping something safe for the future"],["Hardware","The physical parts of a computer"],["Copyright","Legal right controlling copying of creative work"]],
f:["Emulators imitate other computer systems.","Emulation helps preserve old games.","Sharing copyrighted games without permission is illegal."],
fun:"A 2023 study found that most classic video games released in the United States can no longer be bought."});

ADDART({id:"speedrunning",t:"Speedrunning",c:"Technology",i:"⏱️",w:"Speedrun",r:["videogames","esports","livestreaming","emulators","gameconsoles","fandom"],
p:[["Finishing games fast","Players race to complete games as quickly as possible."],["Glitches and skill","Clever tricks can skip whole levels."],["Charity marathons","Events raise millions for good causes."]],
l:"Speedrunning is the practice of playing a video game, or part of one, as fast as possible, usually to set a record. Speedrunners compete in categories with different rules, such as ‘any%’, finishing the game by any means, ‘100%’, collecting everything, or ‘glitchless’, which bans exploiting bugs. Runs are timed and usually recorded or streamed so others can verify them, and leaderboards on websites such as Speedrun.com track records for tens of thousands of games. Top speedrunners practice for thousands of hours, memorizing routes and mastering precise movements, and many discover glitches, unexpected bugs, that let them skip sections, pass through walls or even jump straight to the ending. Speedrunning grew from early video game high scores and spread widely with the internet and live-streaming platforms such as Twitch. It is also known for charity events: Games Done Quick, a week-long marathon of speedruns held twice a year in the United States, has raised tens of millions of dollars for charities such as Doctors Without Borders, and similar events take place in Europe, Japan and elsewhere. Some runs of classic games such as Super Mario Bros. have been optimized to within fractions of a second of what is thought possible.",
s:[["Categories","Besides any% and 100%, categories include ‘low%’, finishing while collecting as little as possible. Tool-assisted speedruns (TAS) use emulators to play frame by frame, showing the theoretical fastest route, and are kept separate from human runs."],["Glitches","In some games, players found ‘arbitrary code execution’, using carefully planned actions to make the game run their own instructions, allowing them to jump to the credits within minutes."],["Games Done Quick","Awesome Games Done Quick, held each January, and Summer Games Done Quick have together raised tens of millions of US dollars for charity since the first event in 2010."],["Community","Speedrunning communities share routes, tricks and tools openly. Moderators verify runs, and cheating scandals, such as spliced videos, have been uncovered by careful analysis."]],
k:[["Goal","Finish a game as fast as possible"],["Common categories","Any%, 100%, glitchless"],["Records tracked on","Leaderboard websites such as Speedrun.com"],["Tool-assisted runs","TAS (separate from human runs)"],["Charity event","Games Done Quick (since 2010)"],["Shared via","Live-streaming"]],
d:[["1993","Doom players share fast completion demos"],["2010","First Games Done Quick event"],["2014","Speedrun.com launches"]],
s2:[["World records","Records in popular games can be broken by tiny margins, sometimes a fraction of a second."],["Not just video games","People also speedrun puzzles such as the Rubik’s Cube, which is called speedcubing."]],
g:[["Speedrun","Playing a game as fast as possible"],["Glitch","An unexpected bug in a game"],["Any%","Finishing by any means"],["Leaderboard","A ranked list of records"]],
f:["Speedrunners try to finish games quickly.","Some speedruns use glitches.","Games Done Quick raises money for charity."],
fun:"Some speedrunners can finish games in minutes that normally take many hours."});

ADDART({id:"twofactor",t:"Two-Factor Authentication",c:"Technology",i:"🔐",w:"Multi-factor authentication",r:["passwords","hacking","phishing","dataprivacy","crypto","smartphone"],
p:[["Two locks are better than one","A second proof of identity besides a password."],["Codes, apps and keys","Text codes, authenticator apps or security keys."],["Stops most attacks","Even a stolen password is not enough."]],
l:"Two-factor authentication (2FA), part of a wider approach called multi-factor authentication (MFA), is a way of protecting online accounts by requiring two different kinds of proof that you are who you say you are. Authentication factors fall into three categories: something you know, such as a password or PIN; something you have, such as a phone or security key; and something you are, such as a fingerprint or face. With 2FA, after entering a password, a person must also provide a second factor, such as a one-time code sent by text message, a code generated by an authenticator app, a tap on a notification, or a physical security key plugged in or tapped on a phone. This means that even if criminals steal or guess a password, for example through a data breach or a phishing email, they still cannot log in without the second factor. Security experts strongly recommend turning on 2FA for important accounts such as email, banking and social media. Codes sent by text message are better than nothing but can be intercepted through ‘SIM swapping’ scams, so authenticator apps and security keys are considered safer. Newer passkeys combine strong security with easier logins.",
s:[["Kinds of second factor","Text-message codes are common and easy but less secure. Authenticator apps generate a new six-digit code every 30 seconds. Hardware security keys use strong cryptography and resist phishing best."],["How effective?","Studies by companies such as Google and Microsoft have found that adding a second factor blocks the vast majority of automated account takeover attempts."],["SIM swapping","In SIM swapping, criminals trick a phone company into moving a victim’s phone number to a new SIM card, letting them receive text-message codes. Using an app or key avoids this."],["Passkeys","Passkeys, supported by major tech companies since 2022, replace passwords with cryptographic keys stored on your devices, unlocked with a fingerprint, face or PIN. They resist phishing and combine two factors in one step."]],
k:[["Short name","2FA (part of MFA)"],["Factor types","Know, have, are"],["Common second factors","Text codes, authenticator apps, security keys"],["Most secure","Hardware security keys and passkeys"],["Risk with text codes","SIM swapping"],["Recommended for","Email, banking, social media"]],
d:[["1980s","Early hardware tokens for secure logins"],["2011","Google offers 2-step verification to all users"],["2014","FIDO Alliance publishes security key standards"],["2022","Major companies announce passkey support"]],
s2:[["Backup codes","When turning on 2FA, services usually give backup codes; storing them safely means you can still log in if you lose your phone."],["Banking","Many banks require two-factor checks for payments, and in Europe ‘strong customer authentication’ rules require it for many online payments."]],
g:[["Authentication","Proving who you are"],["Factor","A type of proof of identity"],["Authenticator app","An app that generates login codes"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing information"]],
f:["2FA requires two kinds of proof.","Authenticator apps are safer than text codes.","2FA protects accounts even if a password is stolen."],
fun:"Authenticator apps create a brand-new login code every 30 seconds."});

ADDART({id:"webaccessibility",t:"Web Accessibility",c:"Technology",i:"♿",w:"Web accessibility",r:["internet","signlang","coding","touchscreens","screens","webcookies"],
p:[["A web for everyone","Websites usable by people with disabilities."],["Screen readers and captions","Tools that read pages aloud and show speech as text."],["Rules and guidelines","WCAG sets international standards."]],
l:"Web accessibility means designing websites, apps and digital content so that everyone, including people with disabilities, can perceive, understand, navigate and interact with them. More than a billion people worldwide live with some form of disability, including visual, hearing, motor and cognitive disabilities, and many more face temporary or age-related difficulties. People use many assistive technologies: blind and low-vision users may use screen readers, which read page text and descriptions aloud, or screen magnifiers; deaf and hard-of-hearing users rely on captions and transcripts; people who cannot use a mouse may navigate by keyboard, switches or voice. Accessible design includes adding text descriptions, called alt text, to images, using clear headings, providing captions for videos, ensuring enough color contrast, making every function usable by keyboard, and writing in plain language. The main international standard is the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), published by the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C), with version 2.2 released in 2023. Many countries require accessibility for government and some business websites, and the European Accessibility Act began applying to many products and services in June 2025. Accessible design often makes websites easier for everyone to use.",
s:[["Screen readers","Screen readers, built into computers and phones such as VoiceOver and TalkBack or available as programs, read text aloud and announce headings, links and buttons. They depend on websites being built with proper structure and labels."],["WCAG principles","WCAG is organized around four principles: content should be perceivable, operable, understandable and robust, often remembered as POUR. It has three levels of conformance: A, AA and AAA."],["Laws","The Americans with Disabilities Act has been applied to websites in US courts. The European Accessibility Act, adopted in 2019, applies from 28 June 2025 to products such as e-books, online shopping and banking services."],["Benefits for all","Captions help people watching videos in noisy places or learning a language. Clear layouts help older people and those using small screens. Keyboard shortcuts help power users."]],
k:[["Goal","Digital content usable by everyone"],["People with disabilities","More than 1 billion worldwide"],["Main standard","WCAG (latest version 2.2, 2023)"],["Assistive tools","Screen readers, captions, magnifiers, voice control"],["Key practices","Alt text, headings, captions, contrast, keyboard access"],["EU law","European Accessibility Act (from June 2025)"]],
d:[["1997","W3C launches the Web Accessibility Initiative"],["1999","WCAG 1.0 published"],["2008","WCAG 2.0 published"],["2023","WCAG 2.2 published"],["2025","European Accessibility Act applies"]],
s2:[["Tim Berners-Lee","The inventor of the World Wide Web, Tim Berners-Lee, said the power of the web is in its universality, and that access by everyone regardless of disability is essential."],["Global Accessibility Awareness Day","Global Accessibility Awareness Day, held on the third Thursday of May, encourages people to learn about digital access."]],
g:[["Accessibility","Being usable by people with disabilities"],["Screen reader","Software that reads screen content aloud"],["Alt text","A text description of an image"],["WCAG","Web Content Accessibility Guidelines"]],
f:["Screen readers read web pages aloud.","WCAG is the main web accessibility standard.","Captions help deaf viewers."],
fun:"Captions and clear layouts designed for accessibility help almost everyone, not just people with disabilities."});
